1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,557
This is ECE 358, video 18. The topic is link-state routing: Dijkstra's algorithm and OSPF.

2
00:00:10,007 --> 00:00:28,631
After this video, you can run Dijkstra's algorithm and fill its table step by step. You can draw the least-cost-path tree and the forwarding table. You can explain link state, OSPF, flooding and link costs. And you can solve question 85 of the question bank.

3
00:00:29,081 --> 00:00:39,556
Routing finds good paths through a network of routers. We use the graph model of slide 79. Each node is a router. Here u is one node.

4
00:00:40,006 --> 00:00:43,931
Each edge is a link. Here is the link between u and x.

5
00:00:44,381 --> 00:00:50,376
The number on an edge is the link cost, c x y. For example, c u x is 1.

6
00:00:50,826 --> 00:00:58,335
A path is a sequence of links from a source to a destination. The cost of a path is the sum of its link costs.

7
00:00:58,785 --> 00:01:04,652
For example, the path u, x, y, z costs 1 plus 1 plus 2, which is 4.

8
00:01:05,102 --> 00:01:11,118
Another path from u to z goes through w. It costs 5 plus 5, which is 10.

9
00:01:11,568 --> 00:01:15,152
4 is less than 10. So the first path is better.

10
00:01:15,602 --> 00:01:24,029
A good path is a least-cost path. The routing algorithm in u must find the least-cost path from u to every other node.

11
00:01:24,479 --> 00:01:30,623
These paths give the forwarding table of u. The green links show the answer that we will compute.

12
00:01:31,073 --> 00:01:40,801
Slide 82 describes Dijkstra's link-state algorithm. It is centralized. Every node knows the whole topology and every link cost.

13
00:01:41,251 --> 00:01:47,928
The purple chips show that each router holds the same map. A link state broadcast gives them this map.

14
00:01:48,378 --> 00:01:53,413
Because all nodes have the same map, each node can run the algorithm alone.

15
00:01:53,863 --> 00:02:04,828
The algorithm computes the least-cost paths from one node, the source, to all other nodes. Here the source is u. The result gives the forwarding table of u.

16
00:02:05,278 --> 00:02:12,681
The algorithm is iterative. After k iterations, it knows the least-cost paths to k destinations.

17
00:02:13,131 --> 00:02:18,912
In the picture, 3 iterations are done. N prime holds u, x, y and w.

18
00:02:19,362 --> 00:02:31,821
Here is the notation. c x y is the direct link cost from x to y. It is infinity when x and y are not neighbors. In the picture, c w v is 3.

19
00:02:32,271 --> 00:02:39,844
D of v is the current estimate of the least cost from the source to v. In the picture, D of w is 3.

20
00:02:40,294 --> 00:02:47,547
p of v is the node before v on that path, the predecessor. In the picture, p of w is y.

21
00:02:47,997 --> 00:02:52,563
N prime is the set of nodes whose least cost is definitely known.

22
00:02:53,013 --> 00:03:01,375
Slide 83 gives the algorithm. Lines 1 and 2 start the initialization. N prime holds only the source u.

23
00:03:01,825 --> 00:03:12,428
Lines 3 to 5: for each neighbor v of u, D of v is the direct cost c u v. Here v gets 2, w gets 5, and x gets 1.

24
00:03:12,878 --> 00:03:21,582
Line 6: for every other node, D of v is infinity. Here y and z. This first estimate may not be the minimum cost.

25
00:03:22,032 --> 00:03:32,272
Then the loop starts. Line 9 finds the node w that is not in N prime and has the smallest D. Here it is x, with D equal to 1.

26
00:03:32,722 --> 00:03:39,954
Line 10 adds w to N prime. Here x joins N prime. The cost of x is now final.

27
00:03:40,404 --> 00:03:50,751
No other path to x can be cheaper. Every other path starts with the link to v or to w. These links already cost 2 and 5, more than 1.

28
00:03:51,201 --> 00:04:02,187
Lines 11 and 12 update the neighbors of w that are not in N prime. The purple links show the neighbors that x checks: v, w and y.

29
00:04:02,637 --> 00:04:10,531
For each such v, D of v becomes the minimum of the old D of v, and D of w plus c w v.

30
00:04:10,981 --> 00:04:17,295
Line 15 repeats the loop until all nodes are in N prime. Each pass adds one node.

31
00:04:17,745 --> 00:04:25,745
There are 6 nodes, and u is in N prime from the start. So the loop runs 6 minus 1, which is 5 times.

32
00:04:26,195 --> 00:04:36,606
Here is the update rule of line 12 in a small picture. At the start, u knows only the direct link to w. So D of w is 5.

33
00:04:37,056 --> 00:04:42,155
Then x joins N prime. Its cost, D of x, is 1, and it is final.

34
00:04:42,605 --> 00:04:51,074
Now a path through x is possible. Its cost is D of x plus c x w. That is 1 plus 3, which is 4.

35
00:04:51,524 --> 00:04:59,801
The minimum of 5 and 4 is 4. So D of w becomes 4, and the predecessor p of w becomes x.

36
00:05:00,251 --> 00:05:07,825
Change D and p only when the new cost is strictly smaller. If the costs are equal, keep the old entry.

37
00:05:08,275 --> 00:05:15,123
Now the example of slide 84. The source is u. Pause the video and try the table first.

38
00:05:15,573 --> 00:05:20,693
Step 0 is the initialization. N prime holds only the source, u.

39
00:05:21,143 --> 00:05:28,247
v is a neighbor of u. D of v is the link cost c u v, which is 2. The predecessor is u.

40
00:05:28,697 --> 00:05:36,910
w is a neighbor of u. D of w is the link cost c u w, which is 5. The predecessor is u.

41
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:44,763
x is a neighbor of u. D of x is the link cost c u x, which is 1. The predecessor is u.

42
00:05:45,213 --> 00:05:49,415
y and z are not neighbors of u. So their D is infinity.

43
00:05:49,865 --> 00:05:59,721
Step 1. The smallest D outside N prime is 1, for x. x joins N prime. The other values are copied to the new row.

44
00:06:00,171 --> 00:06:09,729
Update v. D of x plus c x v is 1 plus 2, which is 3. That is not smaller than 2. So v keeps 2 comma u.

45
00:06:10,179 --> 00:06:20,739
Update w. D of x plus c x w is 1 plus 3, which is 4. That is smaller than 5. So w becomes 4 comma x.

46
00:06:21,189 --> 00:06:30,981
Update y. D of x plus c x y is 1 plus 1, which is 2. That is smaller than infinity. So y becomes 2 comma x.

47
00:06:31,431 --> 00:06:51,825
Step 2. The smallest D outside N prime is 2, for y. v and y both have D equal to 2. This is a tie. The slide picks y. Ties can be broken in any order; the final costs do not change. y joins N prime. The other values are copied to the new row.

48
00:06:52,275 --> 00:07:02,750
Update w. D of y plus c y w is 2 plus 1, which is 3. That is smaller than 4. So w becomes 3 comma y.

49
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:13,163
Update z. D of y plus c y z is 2 plus 2, which is 4. That is smaller than infinity. So z becomes 4 comma y.

50
00:07:13,613 --> 00:07:23,426
Step 3. The smallest D outside N prime is 2, for v. v joins N prime. The other values are copied to the new row.

51
00:07:23,876 --> 00:07:34,628
Update w. D of v plus c v w is 2 plus 3, which is 5. That is not smaller than 3. So w keeps 3 comma y.

52
00:07:35,078 --> 00:07:45,190
Step 4. The smallest D outside N prime is 3, for w. w joins N prime. The other values are copied to the new row.

53
00:07:45,640 --> 00:07:56,264
Update z. D of w plus c w z is 3 plus 5, which is 8. That is not smaller than 4. So z keeps 4 comma y.

54
00:07:56,714 --> 00:08:03,135
Step 5. The smallest D outside N prime is 4, for z. z joins N prime.

55
00:08:03,585 --> 00:08:14,615
All nodes are in N prime, so the algorithm stops. The circled values are the final costs: v 2, w 3, x 1, y 2, z 4.

56
00:08:15,065 --> 00:08:23,086
Now build the least-cost-path tree. Trace the predecessors back from each node. Start with z: p of z is y.

57
00:08:23,536 --> 00:08:25,008
p of y is x.

58
00:08:25,458 --> 00:08:27,335
p of x is u, the source.

59
00:08:27,785 --> 00:08:36,447
So the path from u to z is u, x, y, z. Its cost is 1 plus 1 plus 2, which is 4. That is D of z.

60
00:08:36,897 --> 00:08:41,974
Do this for every node. The green links join each node to its predecessor.

61
00:08:42,424 --> 00:08:53,752
Remove all other links. The result is the least-cost-path tree from u. It has 6 nodes and 5 links: one link to the predecessor of each node except u.

62
00:08:54,202 --> 00:09:04,570
Now the forwarding table of u. It holds only the first link of each path. For v, the path is u, v. So the outgoing link is u v.

63
00:09:05,020 --> 00:09:13,639
Every other destination, x, y, w and z, is in the subtree below x. So all of them use the link u x.

64
00:09:14,089 --> 00:09:24,627
One small error on slide 85: the table lists destination x twice. The last row is for z. It also uses the link u x.

65
00:09:25,077 --> 00:09:34,080
Here is a second example, from slides 86 and 92. The source is again u. Pause the video and try the table first.

66
00:09:34,530 --> 00:09:39,650
Step 0 is the initialization. N prime holds only the source, u.

67
00:09:40,100 --> 00:09:47,460
v is a neighbor of u. D of v is the link cost c u v, which is 7. The predecessor is u.

68
00:09:47,910 --> 00:09:56,145
w is a neighbor of u. D of w is the link cost c u w, which is 3. The predecessor is u.

69
00:09:56,595 --> 00:10:04,040
x is a neighbor of u. D of x is the link cost c u x, which is 5. The predecessor is u.

70
00:10:04,490 --> 00:10:08,693
y and z are not neighbors of u. So their D is infinity.

71
00:10:09,143 --> 00:10:19,276
Step 1. The smallest D outside N prime is 3, for w. w joins N prime. The other values are copied to the new row.

72
00:10:19,726 --> 00:10:30,478
Update v. D of w plus c w v is 3 plus 3, which is 6. That is smaller than 7. So v becomes 6 comma w.

73
00:10:30,928 --> 00:10:41,616
Update x. D of w plus c w x is 3 plus 4, which is 7. That is not smaller than 5. So x keeps 5 comma u.

74
00:10:42,066 --> 00:10:53,735
Update y. D of w plus c w y is 3 plus 8, which is 11. That is smaller than infinity. So y becomes 11 comma w.

75
00:10:54,185 --> 00:11:04,084
Step 2. The smallest D outside N prime is 5, for x. x joins N prime. The other values are copied to the new row.

76
00:11:04,534 --> 00:11:15,627
Update y. D of x plus c x y is 5 plus 7, which is 12. That is not smaller than 11. So y keeps 11 comma w.

77
00:11:16,077 --> 00:11:26,851
Update z. D of x plus c x z is 5 plus 9, which is 14. That is smaller than infinity. So z becomes 14 comma x.

78
00:11:27,301 --> 00:11:37,029
Step 3. The smallest D outside N prime is 6, for v. v joins N prime. The other values are copied to the new row.

79
00:11:37,479 --> 00:11:47,484
Update y. D of v plus c v y is 6 plus 4, which is 10. That is smaller than 11. So y becomes 10 comma v.

80
00:11:47,934 --> 00:11:57,662
Step 4. The smallest D outside N prime is 10, for y. y joins N prime. The other values are copied to the new row.

81
00:11:58,112 --> 00:12:08,309
Update z. D of y plus c y z is 10 plus 2, which is 12. That is smaller than 14. So z becomes 12 comma y.

82
00:12:08,759 --> 00:12:15,415
Step 5. The smallest D outside N prime is 12, for z. z joins N prime.

83
00:12:15,865 --> 00:12:27,108
All nodes are in N prime, so the algorithm stops. The circled values are the final costs: v 6, w 3, x 5, y 10, z 12.

84
00:12:27,558 --> 00:12:41,233
Read the predecessors from the table: p of v is w, p of w is u, p of x is u, p of y is v, and p of z is y. The green links join each node to its predecessor.

85
00:12:41,683 --> 00:12:49,149
Remove the other links. This is the tree of slide 92. The slide draws it in red; here it is green.

86
00:12:49,599 --> 00:13:02,143
The routing table of u needs the first hop of each path. For z, the path is u, w, v, y, z. Its cost is 3 plus 3 plus 4 plus 2, which is 12.

87
00:13:02,593 --> 00:13:05,729
So the outgoing link for z is u w.

88
00:13:06,179 --> 00:13:13,027
v, w and y are also below w in the tree. They also use the link u w.

89
00:13:13,477 --> 00:13:19,216
Only x is reached by the link u x. Slide 93 shows the same table.

90
00:13:19,666 --> 00:13:29,543
Slide 92 also says: run Dijkstra's algorithm on all other nodes. Each router computes its own tree and its own routing table.

91
00:13:29,993 --> 00:13:44,223
OSPF means Open Shortest Path First. It is the link-state protocol for routing inside one autonomous system, an A S. Slide 122 says that it is widely used.

92
00:13:44,673 --> 00:13:58,198
In step 1, each router floods OSPF advertisements, also called L S advertisements. So every router learns the complete topology of the network inside the A S.

93
00:13:58,648 --> 00:14:09,571
In step 2, each router, for example u, runs Dijkstra's algorithm on this topology. It finds the shortest paths from itself to all other nodes.

94
00:14:10,021 --> 00:14:18,469
In step 3, each router finds the next hop for each destination from those paths. These next hops fill its routing table.

95
00:14:18,919 --> 00:14:23,228
The link state of a router is the list of its own links and their costs.

96
00:14:23,678 --> 00:14:29,417
All routers build the same map from these lists. So their shortest paths agree with each other.

97
00:14:29,867 --> 00:14:39,061
Slide 88 lists three types of links in OSPF. The first is a point-to-point link. It connects two routers directly.

98
00:14:39,511 --> 00:14:51,181
The second is a transient link, also called a broadcast link. It is a LAN that connects two or more routers. Traffic can enter the LAN through one router and leave through another.

99
00:14:51,631 --> 00:15:00,057
The third is a stub link. It is a LAN with only one router. Traffic to or from this LAN always goes through that one router.

100
00:15:00,507 --> 00:15:09,681
A router includes all of these links in its L S advertisement. So the other routers learn which destinations are connected in the A S.

101
00:15:10,131 --> 00:15:23,507
Slide 89 shows the link state of each router in a five-router network. The link state of A lists its own links: A to B with cost 5, A to C with cost 2, and A to D with cost 3.

102
00:15:23,957 --> 00:15:30,335
The link state of B: B to A cost 5, B to C cost 4, and B to E cost 3.

103
00:15:30,785 --> 00:15:36,759
The link state of C: C to A cost 2, C to B cost 4, and C to E cost 4.

104
00:15:37,209 --> 00:15:41,838
D has only one link. Its link state is D to A with cost 3.

105
00:15:42,288 --> 00:15:47,088
The link state of E: E to B cost 3, and E to C cost 4.

106
00:15:47,538 --> 00:15:53,831
Each router collects the link states of all routers. Together, the five lists give the full map.

107
00:15:54,281 --> 00:16:05,460
The lists hold 3 plus 3 plus 3 plus 1 plus 2, which is 12 entries. The network has 6 links. So each link appears twice, once from each end.

108
00:16:05,910 --> 00:16:09,537
With this map, each router runs Dijkstra's algorithm.

109
00:16:09,987 --> 00:16:20,803
Slide 90 shows how one link state reaches every router. This is called flooding. Router D creates its advertisement. It holds D, A, 3.

110
00:16:21,253 --> 00:16:27,482
D sends the advertisement on all of its links. D has only one link, so A receives it.

111
00:16:27,932 --> 00:16:32,625
A sends a copy on all of its other links. So B and C receive it.

112
00:16:33,075 --> 00:16:37,193
B and C send copies on their other links, so E receives it.

113
00:16:37,643 --> 00:16:47,307
B and C also send copies to each other. A router that already has this advertisement does not send it again. So the flooding stops.

114
00:16:47,757 --> 00:16:55,458
The link state of every other node is flooded in the same way. At the end, every router has the same complete map.

115
00:16:55,908 --> 00:17:10,116
What is the cost of a link? Slide 91 gives the Cisco default. The cost is inversely proportional to the link speed. Cost equals 100 Mbps divided by the link speed.

116
00:17:10,566 --> 00:17:16,774
A 1 Mbps link has cost 100 divided by 1, which is 100.

117
00:17:17,224 --> 00:17:22,941
A 10 Mbps link has cost 100 divided by 10, which is 10.

118
00:17:23,391 --> 00:17:35,423
A 100 Mbps link has cost 100 divided by 100, which is 1. So faster links get smaller costs, and Dijkstra's algorithm prefers them.

119
00:17:35,873 --> 00:17:49,313
1 Gbps is 1000 Mbps. 100 divided by 1000 is 0 point 1. But the slide gives cost 1 for a 1 Gbps link.

120
00:17:49,763 --> 00:17:53,390
Routers can also be configured with other cost methods.

121
00:17:53,840 --> 00:18:07,003
Now the question bank. Questions 81 to 84 are short descriptive questions. Question 81: what is the main function of OSPF? Pause the video and try it.

122
00:18:07,453 --> 00:18:21,277
OSPF does routing inside one A S. Each router learns the topology by flooding. It runs Dijkstra's algorithm. Then it fills its routing table with the next hop to each destination.

123
00:18:21,727 --> 00:18:37,407
Question 82: what cost metrics can OSPF use? The operator sets the link cost. The default on the slide is 100 Mbps divided by the link speed. Other methods can be configured.

124
00:18:37,857 --> 00:18:52,961
Question 83: explain the link state of a router. It is the list of the router's own links and their costs. For example, the link state of A is A B 5, A C 2, and A D 3. It is flooded to all routers.

125
00:18:53,411 --> 00:19:01,581
Question 84: which graph algorithm does OSPF use? It uses Dijkstra's shortest-path algorithm.

126
00:19:02,031 --> 00:19:12,591
Question 85 of the question bank. Run Dijkstra's algorithm to find the shortest-path tree rooted at node z. Then build the routing table on z.

127
00:19:13,041 --> 00:19:19,740
This is the same graph as slide 84, but now the source is z. Pause the video and try it.

128
00:19:20,190 --> 00:19:25,203
Step 0 is the initialization. N prime holds only the source, z.

129
00:19:25,653 --> 00:19:34,101
w is a neighbor of z. D of w is the link cost c z w, which is 5. The predecessor is z.

130
00:19:34,551 --> 00:19:42,018
y is a neighbor of z. D of y is the link cost c z y, which is 2. The predecessor is z.

131
00:19:42,468 --> 00:19:46,991
u, v and x are not neighbors of z. So their D is infinity.

132
00:19:47,441 --> 00:19:57,019
Step 1. The smallest D outside N prime is 2, for y. y joins N prime. The other values are copied to the new row.

133
00:19:57,469 --> 00:20:08,029
Update w. D of y plus c y w is 2 plus 1, which is 3. That is smaller than 5. So w becomes 3 comma y.

134
00:20:08,479 --> 00:20:18,442
Update x. D of y plus c y x is 2 plus 1, which is 3. That is smaller than infinity. So x becomes 3 comma y.

135
00:20:18,892 --> 00:20:39,223
Step 2. The smallest D outside N prime is 3, for w. w and x both have D equal to 3. This is a tie. We take w first, by name. The other order gives the same final result. w joins N prime. The other values are copied to the new row.

136
00:20:39,673 --> 00:20:50,617
Update u. D of w plus c w u is 3 plus 5, which is 8. That is smaller than infinity. So u becomes 8 comma w.

137
00:20:51,067 --> 00:21:02,053
Update v. D of w plus c w v is 3 plus 3, which is 6. That is smaller than infinity. So v becomes 6 comma w.

138
00:21:02,503 --> 00:21:13,191
Update x. D of w plus c w x is 3 plus 3, which is 6. That is not smaller than 3. So x keeps 3 comma y.

139
00:21:13,641 --> 00:21:23,604
Step 3. The smallest D outside N prime is 3, for x. x joins N prime. The other values are copied to the new row.

140
00:21:24,054 --> 00:21:33,590
Update u. D of x plus c x u is 3 plus 1, which is 4. That is smaller than 8. So u becomes 4 comma x.

141
00:21:34,040 --> 00:21:43,747
Update v. D of x plus c x v is 3 plus 2, which is 5. That is smaller than 6. So v becomes 5 comma x.

142
00:21:44,197 --> 00:21:53,733
Step 4. The smallest D outside N prime is 4, for u. u joins N prime. The other values are copied to the new row.

143
00:21:54,183 --> 00:22:03,783
Update v. D of u plus c u v is 4 plus 2, which is 6. That is not smaller than 5. So v keeps 5 comma x.

144
00:22:04,233 --> 00:22:10,633
Step 5. The smallest D outside N prime is 5, for v. v joins N prime.

145
00:22:11,083 --> 00:22:22,155
All nodes are in N prime, so the algorithm stops. The circled values are the final costs: u 4, v 5, w 3, x 3, y 2.

146
00:22:22,605 --> 00:22:32,162
Trace the predecessors from the table: p of u is x, p of v is x, p of w is y, p of x is y, and p of y is z.

147
00:22:32,612 --> 00:22:39,545
Remove the other links. This is the shortest-path tree rooted at z. It has 6 nodes and 5 links.

148
00:22:39,995 --> 00:22:49,617
The routing table on z. Every path in the tree starts with the link z y. So every destination uses the outgoing link z y.

149
00:22:50,067 --> 00:22:55,187
Check the surprising row. z has a direct link to w with cost 5.

150
00:22:55,637 --> 00:23:04,469
But the path z, y, w costs 2 plus 1, which is 3. 3 is less than 5, so w also uses z y.

151
00:23:04,919 --> 00:23:14,135
Here is the method for an exam question. One: write row 0 from the direct links of the source. Write infinity for the other nodes.

152
00:23:14,585 --> 00:23:20,729
Two: pick the smallest D that is not in N prime, and circle it. Ties can go in any order.

153
00:23:21,179 --> 00:23:30,416
Three: update only the neighbors of the new node that are not in N prime. Change D and p only when the new cost is strictly smaller.

154
00:23:30,866 --> 00:23:36,903
Four: trace the predecessors to get the tree. Take the first link of each path for the table.

155
00:23:37,353 --> 00:23:47,060
Common errors. First: writing the predecessor as the next hop. In slide 84, p of z is y, but the next hop from u is x.

156
00:23:47,510 --> 00:23:51,009
Second: updating a node that is already in N prime.

157
00:23:51,459 --> 00:23:54,317
Third: changing p when the costs are equal.

158
00:23:54,767 --> 00:24:02,405
Fourth: forgetting that a two-hop path can be cheaper than a direct link, as z to w in question 85.

159
00:24:02,855 --> 00:24:18,151
To check your answer, add the link costs along each tree path. The sum must equal the final D of that node. For example, u, x, y, z costs 1 plus 1 plus 2, which is 4, the final D of z.

160
00:24:18,601 --> 00:24:27,859
Here is the recap. In link-state routing, every router knows the full topology and all link costs, from flooded advertisements.

161
00:24:28,309 --> 00:24:35,563
Dijkstra's algorithm adds one node to N prime in each step. It updates D of v with the minimum rule.

162
00:24:36,013 --> 00:24:42,114
Trace the predecessors to get the tree. The forwarding table holds the first link of each path.

163
00:24:42,564 --> 00:24:54,809
OSPF uses this method inside an A S: flood, run Dijkstra, find the next hop, and fill the routing table. Its links are point-to-point, transient, or stub.

